    ichiTitle: 'Ichimoku Kinko Hyo',
    ichiIntro: 'A Japanese \'one-glance equilibrium chart\' created by Goichi Hosoda. Five lines and a projected \'cloud\' show trend, support/resistance, momentum and entry signals all at once — designed so you can read the market at a glance. Very popular in forex and crypto.',
    ichiLinesTitle: 'The 5 lines',
    ichiLinesIntro: 'Ichimoku plots five lines derived only from highs and lows (the main lines don\'t use closing prices). Together they define trend and key levels.',
    ichiTenkanName: 'Tenkan-sen (Conversion line)',
    ichiTenkanDesc: 'Average of the highest high and lowest low over the last 9 periods. A fast line that tracks short-term momentum; acts as minor support/resistance.',
    ichiKijunName: 'Kijun-sen (Base line)',
    ichiKijunDesc: 'Average of the high/low over the last 26 periods. Marks the medium-term trend and a strong support/resistance level; price often pulls back to it.',
    ichiSenkouAName: 'Senkou Span A (Leading Span A)',
    ichiSenkouADesc: 'The midpoint of Tenkan and Kijun, plotted 26 periods into the future. Forms one edge of the cloud.',
    ichiSenkouBName: 'Senkou Span B (Leading Span B)',
    ichiSenkouBDesc: 'The midpoint of the 52-period high/low, plotted 26 periods ahead. The other, slower edge of the cloud.',
    ichiChikouName: 'Chikou Span (Lagging line)',
    ichiChikouDesc: 'The current close plotted 26 periods into the past. If it is above past price the trend is strong; it confirms (or vetoes) signals.',
    ichiCloudTitle: 'The Kumo (cloud)',
    ichiCloudIntro: 'The area between Senkou Span A and B, projected ahead. It is Ichimoku\'s dynamic support/resistance zone and the fastest way to read the trend.',
    ichiAboveName: 'Price above the cloud',
    ichiAboveDesc: 'Uptrend. The cloud below acts as support. Favour long setups.',
    ichiBelowName: 'Price below the cloud',
    ichiBelowDesc: 'Downtrend. The cloud above acts as resistance. Favour short setups.',
    ichiInsideName: 'Price inside the cloud',
    ichiInsideDesc: 'No clear trend / consolidation. Signals are unreliable — many traders stay out.',
    ichiThickName: 'Cloud thickness & colour',
    ichiThickDesc: 'A thick cloud = strong support/resistance that is harder to break; a thin cloud = weak. The colour flips when Span A crosses Span B, hinting at a possible trend change.',
    ichiSignalsTitle: 'Main signals',
    ichiTkCrossName: 'Tenkan / Kijun cross (TK cross)',
    ichiTkCrossDesc: 'Tenkan crossing above Kijun is bullish; below is bearish. Strongest when it happens above the cloud (bullish) or below it (bearish), in the trend\'s direction.',
    ichiCloudBreakName: 'Cloud breakout',
    ichiCloudBreakDesc: 'Price closing above the cloud flips the bias bullish; closing below flips it bearish. A core trend-following signal.',
    ichiChikouConfName: 'Chikou confirmation',
    ichiChikouConfDesc: 'A signal is stronger when the Chikou span is also clear of past price in the same direction (above price for longs, below for shorts).',
    ichiTipsTitle: 'Tips & common mistakes',
    ichiTip1: 'The default 9/26/52 settings come from the old 6-day trading week. They work well on daily charts; adjust them with care on other timeframes.',
    ichiTip2: 'Don\'t trade against the cloud. Taking longs below the cloud (or shorts above it) fights the trend and fails often.',
    ichiTip3: 'Wait for confluence: TK cross + price on the right side of the cloud + Chikou confirming. One line alone is not a system.',
    ewTitle: 'Elliott Wave Theory',
    ewIntro: 'Ralph Elliott observed that crowd psychology moves markets in repetitive wave patterns. Price advances in a 5-wave \'motive\' sequence in the direction of the trend, then corrects in a 3-wave sequence against it. The same 5-3 shape repeats at every degree, from minutes to decades.',
    ewMotiveTitle: 'The 5 motive waves (impulse)',
    ewMotiveIntro: 'Five waves that push price in the direction of the main trend. Waves 1, 3 and 5 advance; waves 2 and 4 are partial pullbacks. Wave 3 is usually the strongest and never the shortest.',
    ewWave1Name: 'Wave 1 — The start',
    ewWave1Desc: 'The first push higher. Often weak and doubted; only a minority sees the trend beginning while most still expect the prior move to resume.',
    ewWave2Name: 'Wave 2 — First pullback',
    ewWave2Desc: 'A correction of wave 1 (often 50–61.8%). Sentiment is still bearish, but it never falls below the start of wave 1.',
    ewWave3Name: 'Wave 3 — The powerhouse',
    ewWave3Desc: 'Usually the longest and strongest wave, driven by broad recognition of the trend. It can never be the shortest of the three impulse waves.',
    ewWave4Name: 'Wave 4 — Rest',
    ewWave4Desc: 'A shallow, often complex correction (typically ~38.2%). It never overlaps the price territory of wave 1.',
    ewWave5Name: 'Wave 5 — The finale',
    ewWave5Desc: 'The last push, driven by the crowd and late buyers. Often shows weakening momentum and divergences that warn of the coming reversal.',
    ewCorrTitle: 'The 3 corrective waves (A-B-C)',
    ewCorrIntro: 'After the 5-wave advance, price corrects in three waves against the trend, labelled A, B and C.',
    ewWaveAName: 'Wave A — First drop',
    ewWaveADesc: 'The initial move against the trend. Often mistaken for a simple pullback while the uptrend is actually ending.',
    ewWaveBName: 'Wave B — The trap',
    ewWaveBDesc: 'A bounce that partially recovers wave A. It fools latecomers into thinking the trend has resumed (\'bull trap\').',
    ewWaveCName: 'Wave C — Capitulation',
    ewWaveCDesc: 'A strong, broad move that usually exceeds the end of wave A, confirming the correction and often ending in capitulation.',
    ewRulesTitle: 'The 3 unbreakable rules',
    ewRule1Name: 'Wave 2 never retraces 100% of wave 1',
    ewRule1Desc: 'If price falls below the origin of wave 1, the count is wrong — it is not wave 2.',
    ewRule2Name: 'Wave 3 is never the shortest',
    ewRule2Desc: 'Among waves 1, 3 and 5, wave 3 can be the longest or the middle one, but never the shortest. It is often the longest.',
    ewRule3Name: 'Wave 4 never overlaps wave 1',
    ewRule3Desc: 'The low of wave 4 must not enter the price range of wave 1 (in a standard impulse). If it does, recount.',
    ewPatternsTitle: 'Corrective patterns',
    ewZigzagName: 'Zigzag (5-3-5)',
    ewZigzagDesc: 'A sharp, deep correction. Waves A and C have five sub-waves; B is short. The most common single correction.',
    ewFlatName: 'Flat (3-3-5)',
    ewFlatDesc: 'A sideways correction where A, B and C are roughly equal. Signals a strong underlying trend that resumes afterwards.',
    ewTriangleName: 'Triangle (3-3-3-3-3)',
    ewTriangleDesc: 'A converging five-wave (A-B-C-D-E) consolidation, usually in wave 4 or B. Precedes a final thrust in the trend direction.',
    ewFibTitle: 'Fibonacci relationships',
    ewFibDesc: 'Waves relate through Fibonacci ratios: wave 2 often retraces 50–61.8% of wave 1; wave 3 extends 161.8% of wave 1; wave 4 retraces ~38.2% of wave 3; wave 5 often equals wave 1 or 61.8% of waves 1–3. These ratios help project targets and validate a count.',
    ewDegreesTitle: 'Wave degrees',
    ewDegreesDesc: 'The same 5-3 structure repeats at every scale (fractal): Grand Supercycle, Supercycle, Cycle, Primary, Intermediate, Minor, Minute, Minuette and Subminuette. A wave 3 on the daily chart is itself made of five smaller waves on the hourly chart.',
    ewMistakesTitle: 'Common mistakes',
    ewMistake1: 'Forcing a wave count to fit your bias instead of what the chart shows. Elliott is interpretive — stay flexible and always have an alternate count.',
    ewMistake2: 'Ignoring the three rules. If wave 2 retraces 100%, wave 3 is the shortest, or wave 4 overlaps wave 1, your count is invalid — recount.',
    ewMistake3: 'Using Elliott alone. Combine it with support/resistance, Fibonacci and momentum for confirmation, and always define invalidation levels.',
