    marketTypesIntro: 'Every market has its own hours, liquidity, volatility and drivers. Understanding what you trade — and how each market behaves — is your first edge: the same strategy can win in one market and fail in another.',
    marketParticipantsIntro: 'Price is the result of a tug-of-war between very different players, each with its own size, horizon and motivation. Knowing who moves the market — and why — helps you read order flow instead of fighting it.',
    sessionsIntro: 'The market trades 24 hours through three regions that hand over to each other: Asia-Pacific, Europe and America. Volatility, spreads and the best instruments change with the clock — and the session overlaps are where most of the volume happens.',
    mktBondsName: 'Bonds / Fixed Income',
    mktBondsDesc: 'Debt issued by governments and companies. The largest market in the world by size; sovereign yields (like the US 10-year) set the \'risk-free\' rate that prices every other asset. Moves inversely to interest rates.',
    mktBondsVolume: '~$140T outstanding',
    mktOptionsName: 'Options',
    mktOptionsDesc: 'Contracts giving the right — not the obligation — to buy (call) or sell (put) an asset at a set price before expiry. Used to hedge risk, generate income or speculate with leverage and defined risk. Their price depends on volatility and time.',
    mktOptionsVolume: '~$50B/day (US)',
    mktCfdsName: 'CFDs',
    mktCfdsDesc: 'Contracts for Difference: you trade an asset\'s price change without owning it, with leverage and both long and short. Popular in Europe for indices, forex and commodities — but leverage amplifies losses and costs (spread/overnight financing) add up.',
    mktCfdsVolume: 'Broker-dependent',
    partBanksName: 'Investment & Commercial Banks',
    partBanksDesc: 'The plumbing of the market: they make markets, execute huge client orders, run proprietary desks and dominate FX. Their research and order flow shape short-term direction.',
    partHedgeFundsName: 'Hedge Funds',
    partHedgeFundsDesc: 'Lightly regulated funds chasing absolute returns with aggressive, flexible strategies (long/short, macro, arbitrage, quant). They can move markets fast and are often the \'smart money\' on the other side of retail trades.',
    partFundsName: 'Pension & Mutual Funds',
    partFundsDesc: 'The largest pools of long-term capital. They invest slowly and in size for retirement and savings; their rebalancing flows and index-inclusion decisions move whole sectors over weeks and months.',
    partHftName: 'HFT & Algorithms',
    partHftDesc: 'High-Frequency Trading firms use ultra-fast algorithms to trade in microseconds, providing much of today\'s liquidity and capturing tiny spreads across millions of orders. They dominate intraday volume.',
    partBrokersName: 'Brokers & Dealers',
    partBrokersDesc: 'The intermediaries that give you access to the market. Some pass your order to the exchange (ECN/STP); others take the other side (market makers/dealers). How they route orders affects your fills and costs.',
    partCorporatesName: 'Corporations & Hedgers',
    partCorporatesDesc: 'Real businesses (airlines, exporters, miners) that use futures and FX not to speculate but to hedge real exposure — fuel, currencies, raw materials. Their steady, non-directional flow is the backbone of commodity and FX markets.',
    sessSydneyName: 'Sydney (Pacific)',
    sessSydneyHours: '22:00–07:00 UTC',
